"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"84d943a6deeb8cc6c6f632531aaf4f1c62275239","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rustsec/advisory-db/commits/84d943a6deeb8cc6c6f632531aaf4f1c62275239","html_url":"https://github.com/rustsec/advisory-db/commit/84d943a6deeb8cc6c6f632531aaf4f1c62275239"}],"stats":{"total":39,"additions":39,"deletions":0},"files":[{"sha":"c97ebd4bf4e20631bb52c3abbf8d6df302c97b4c","filename":"crates/crypto-bigint/RUSTSEC-0000-0000.md","status":"added","additions":39,"deletions":0,"changes":39,"blob_url":"https://github.com/rustsec/advisory-db/blob/a32530a9217e93567241cd7d6d43352c5403ec8f/crates%2Fcrypto-bigint%2FRUSTSEC-0000-0000.md","raw_url":"https://github.com/rustsec/advisory-db/raw/a32530a9217e93567241cd7d6d43352c5403ec8f/crates%2Fcrypto-bigint%2FRUSTSEC-0000-0000.md","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rustsec/advisory-db/contents/crates%2Fcrypto-bigint%2FRUSTSEC-0000-0000.md?ref=a32530a9217e93567241cd7d6d43352c5403ec8f","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,39 @@\n+```toml\n+[advisory]\n+id = \"RUSTSEC-0000-0000\"\n+package = \"crypto-bigint\"\n+date = \"2026-10-23\"\n+url = \"https://github.com/RustCrypto/crypto-bigint/pull/1365\"\n+categories = [\"crypto-failure\"]\n+license = \"CC-BY-4.0\"\n+\n+[versions]\n+patched = [\">= 0.7.6\"]\n+```\n+\n+# Multiple timing variability issues in operations intended to be constant-time\n+\n+## Summary\n+\n+Several operations intended to be constant-time could execute secret-dependent\n+branches, depending on the compiler and target CPU. These include subtraction,\n+zero counting, division, and Montgomery multiplication.\n+\n+Additionally, `UintRef` equality could terminate at the first unequal limb,\n+and `BoxedMontyForm::invert` branched on whether an inverse exists.\n+\n+## Impact\n+\n+Applications using affected operations on secret integers may leak information\n+through timing or microarchitectural side channels. This includes information\n+about intermediate arithmetic values, matching limbs, and invertibility.\n+\n+Such leakage could potentially enable recovery of cryptographic secrets.\n+Practical exploitability depends on the downstream algorithm, compiler, target\n+CPU, and an attacker's ability to observe the side channel.\n+\n+## Resolution\n+\n+Upgrade to v0.7.6, which removes the identified branches, updates `ctutils` to\n+include its constant-time fixes, and adds BINSEC-based regression checks using\n+`cargo checkct` for arithmetic on multiple targets."}]}